rankin@pactechdata.com (Pat Rankin) writes:
> In article <861xwkf5es.fsf@strackenz.spod-central.org>,\
>  psmith@spod-central.org writes...
> > Mark Adamson <mark@nb.net> writes:
> >> Q:  What is NOT extinctable?  Gremlins? WoY? Trolls?
> [...]
> >> and named demons and devils
> >
> > 'extinct' means something different for unique monsters (it's the flag
> > that tracks whether they've already been created).
> 
>      Is that actually a different meaning?  It seems to me that it
> means "don't create another one" in both cases.  Things were different
> back when extinction was based on the number killed rather than the
> number created.

Well, they're _handled_ differently from time to time; on creating a
special level, for example, G_EXTINCT unique monsters won't have a
random replacement, while G_EXTINCT non-uniques will. There are
certainly cases which rely on the two using the same flag, though, so
you can look at it as just a difference in usage.

(My interpretation lets me say generally that non-randomly-generated
monsters won't be extinguished, which is a useful principle :-) Shades
of the "1 defined non-prime" thread of a while back.)

